          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does asthma guidance describe as the stepwise approach, and what changed about reliever choice?",
          "answer": "Current asthma guidance no longer supports using a short-acting reliever alone. The main change is that every adult and adolescent with asthma should receive therapy that includes an inhaled corticosteroid. The guidance says reliever-only treatment leaves the underlying inflammation untreated and is linked to severe flare-ups, even when symptoms are infrequent. Needing several reliever inhalers a year is also described as a sign of higher risk. (Source: *Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy*.)\n\nThe guidance describes treatment in steps. The step is chosen based on current symptom control and flare-up history, and it is reviewed over time rather than fixed. The passage I retrieved does not list what each step contains, so I can't describe the steps themselves.\n\nThe retrieved text also doesn't say exactly how the choice of reliever changed. It covers the shift to always including an inhaled corticosteroid, not a specific swap of one reliever for another. For details on the steps or on which inhalers are used, a clinician or pharmacist can explain what applies to a particular person.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
